{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad5b708966c329c3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9012\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing DAG edge BRANCH-A-E006 -> BRANCH-A-E009: Rule 6 requires repo-onboarding\n10\t    to precede the general implementation of the onboarded component (code needs repo).\n11\t    A-E009 acceptance criteria require the productized image to build from the midstream\n12\t    repository created by A-E006, but its dependencies list only E001 and A-E007,\n13\t    and the summary DAG and justification table omit the edge. Add RHAISTRAT-9012-BRANCH-A-E006\n14\t    to the A-E009 dependencies list and to the diagram.'\n15\t- severity: critical\n16\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n17\t  description: 'Missing DAG edge BRANCH-B-E006 -> BRANCH-B-E009: same Rule 6 violation\n18\t    in the fallback branch — B-E009 builds the productized SeaweedFS image from the\n19\t    midstream repository created by B-E006 but lists only E001 and B-E007 as dependencies.\n20\t    Add RHAISTRAT-9012-BRANCH-B-E006 to the B-E009 dependencies list and to the diagram.'\n21\terror: null\n22\t---\n23\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9012\n24\t\n25\t## Review Summary\n26\t\n27\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n28\tRecommendation: revise\n29\t\n30\tContract validator: OK (body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last\n31\tcoverage, and priority inheritance all passed). Triage consistency: `triage: null`\n32\t(full decomposition) with 5 main-plan epics plus conditional branches — consistent,\n33\tno triage contradiction.\n34\t\n35\tThe decomposition is strong on almost every dimension, but fails on one structural\n36\tdefect: both conditional branches omit the Rule 6 edge from repo-onboarding to the\n37\tgeneral implementation (image productization) epic, so the image epic can be\n38\tscheduled before the repository it builds from exists. DAG Coherence scores 0 and\n39\tthe auto-fail rule applies.\n40\t\n41\t## Criterion Details\n42\t\n43\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n44\tAll four HLRs map to at least one epic: HLR-1 → E001; HLR-2 → E002 plus both\n45\tbranch onboarding chains (the branches are mutually exclusive alternatives, and\n46\tHLR-2's productized-images portion is covered in either outcome); HLR-3 → E003\n47\t(Pipelines) and E004 (model serving), split by component; HLR-4 → E005 (covered,\n48\tnot deferred). Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) blocks only P0 work;\n49\tbranch epics blocking P0 image work are themselves P0; E005 is a docs-authoring\n50\tepic and its P1 (Major → P1) follows the documented docs-authoring priority\n51\texception. No priority collapse: every epic maps to HLRs at a single priority\n52\tlevel, and the E003/E004 split follows component boundaries, not priority labels.\n53\t\n54\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n55\tDiagram and frontmatter are mutually consistent (every arrow in the summary DAG\n56\thas a matching `dependencies` entry and vice versa; arrows point dependency →\n57\tdependent). Rule 7 (license-validation ∥ repo-onboarding), Rule 8\n58\t(license-validation ∥ konflux-onboarding), and Rule 10 (konflux-onboarding ∥\n59\tgeneral implementation) parallelism are all respected — no edge between A/B-E008\n60\tand A/B-E009, and the \"build pipeline green\" AC on both image epics carries the\n61\tcoupling as required. Rule 13 is handled correctly: the image digest consumed by\n62\tE002's operator packaging and the store endpoint consumed by E003/E004 are treated\n63\tas configuration references with AC gates, not `dependencies` edges. Branch files\n64\tall set both `branch` and `gated_by` (present in their direct `dependencies`), and\n65\tno main-plan epic depends on a branch epic.\n66\t\n67\tHowever, there is a missing edge discoverable from epic content, in **both**\n68\tbranches:\n69\t\n70\t- **BRANCH-A-E006 → BRANCH-A-E009** and **BRANCH-B-E006 → BRANCH-B-E009**.\n71\t  Rule 6 states repo-onboarding → general implementation of the onboarded\n72\t  component is *always serial* (\"code needs repo\"). A/B-E009's acceptance\n73\t  criteria require a productized image that \"builds from the midstream\n74\t  repository\" created by A/B-E006, and its scope is \"built from the midstream\n75\t  repository through Konflux\" — a build-time artifact dependency on the\n76\t  repository, not a configuration reference. Yet A/B-E009's `dependencies` list\n77\t  only E001 and A/B-E007, and the summary DAG omits the edge (and its\n78\t  justification table never addresses it). The asymmetry is visible in the same\n79\t  branch: A/B-E008 depends on E006 precisely because \"Konflux onboarding needs\n80\t  the midstream repo to exist\" — the image build needs it to exist just as much.\n81\t  A missing edge where a data/artifact dependency exists is a critical issue;\n82\t  this criterion scores 0.\n83\t\n84\tCritical path length (3) is within range for the strategy's size and does not\n85\tchange with the missing edges added.\n86\t\n87\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n88\tDistinct component/team tuples produce separate epics (E003 → Data Science\n89\tPipelines / RHOAI Pipelines; E004 → Serving Runtimes / RHOAI Model Serving; E005\n90\t→ Documentation / RHOAI Documentation). The bundled store is a new component\n91\twith no canonical name; assigning all store-productization epics to Platform\n92\tOperator is documented in the Component Mapping Notes and passes the canonical\n93\tcomponent check. No scope duplication: E003 and E004 are analogous defaults in\n94\tdifferent components/repos (not two presentation modes of one surface), E002\n95\t(operator enablement) and A/B-E009 (image productization) are different work\n96\tproducts, and the A/B branches are mutually exclusive alternatives. No epic\n97\tbundles code implementation with content authoring (E005 is pure docs). Epic\n98\tsizes are plausibly single-sprint.\n99\t\n100\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n101\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (MinIO cleared vs Apache-2.0\n102\tfallback) determines which branch's epic set exists, and it has downstream\n103\tdependents. E002's gate is real (`gated_by: E001`, in direct `dependencies`,\n104\t`gate_failure_impact` with action `rewrite` and a concrete fallback — the\n105\tenablement surface re-targets to the fallback store). All eight branch epics set\n106\t`branch`, `gated_by: E001` (in direct dependencies), and `gate_failure_impact`\n107\t(action `remove`/`rewrite` with mirror-branch fallbacks). Every epic listing the\n108\tInvestigation in `dependencies` also sets `gated_by`. E003/E004 deliberately do\n109\tnot gate on E001, with a documented and sound justification (both candidates\n110\texpose the same S3-compatible API; the store identity is a configuration\n111\tdetail). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n112\t\n113\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n114\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: E001 carries\n115\t`investigation_signals` with a 5-signal rationale file; all Implementation epics\n116\tcarry `ai_signals` with 9-signal rationale files. E001's values fit the rubric\n117\t(concrete pass/fail question → `question_specificity: 1`; answer is a pending\n118\thuman legal decision → `source_accessibility: 0` and `human_judgment_required:\n119\t-2`). Implementation values are consistent with strategy content and the\n120\tsummary's health warnings (e.g. `architecture_claims: -1` on E002/E004 and both\n121\tE009s where no architecture context exists; `pattern_precedent: -1` on B-E009\n122\tversus `0` on A-E009, justified by the less-proven fallback upstream). Rationale\n123\tfiles match the frontmatter values.\n124\t\n125\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n126\tEvery epic has testable ACs. Rule-mandated ACs are present on every epic that\n127\tmeets the rule's criteria: E002 (a replacement of the ad hoc field workaround)\n128\thas the opt-in/off-by-default and documented disable/rollback AC; both A-E009\n129\tand B-E009 carry the Rule 25 \"Konflux build pipeline green\" AC; E005 has the\n130\ttechnical-review-against-implementation AC; E002 carries the \"pinned image\n131\tdigest available\" configuration-reference AC in place of a forbidden edge. No\n132\tschedule/timing constraints are misfiled as ACs (the legal-review turnaround and\n133\tupstream release cadence notes are correctly in Risks/Notes). No bare draft IDs\n134\tin epic bodies — sibling work is referenced by role (\"the Platform Operator\n135\tpackaging\", \"the Apache-2.0 fallback branch\"), and full epic IDs appear only in\n136\tfrontmatter `dependencies`.\n137\t\n138\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n139\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: the bundled supported store (E002 + branch\n140\tchains), legal clearance with documented derivative-work posture (E001),\n141\tpipelines and model-serving defaults (E003/E004), documentation including the\n142\tstrategy's out-of-scope boundary statements (E005). The strategy's open question\n143\tis fully bounded by exactly two conditional branches matching its stated\n144\toutcomes. Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the\n145\tunpatched/unsupported field-workaround motivation appears in E002's and both\n146\tE009s' scope; the legal-turnaround and stranded-work risks are in the summary's\n147\tHealth Warnings. The Dashboard (listed in Affected Components but with no HLR)\n148\tis classified passive with an explicit validation AC on E003 and an ambiguity\n149\tflag for the strategy author — a defensible, documented interpretation rather\n150\tthan a silent drop. A docs-authoring epic exists for the documentation\n151\trequirement (HLR-4).\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9605b1b4cb9a760f","agent_id":"a8de1ef08bb0f2983","session_id":"44316181-c1df-4de5-a431-6d099bdfeb64","uuid":"199a02cd-d619-41b2-8606-60fab8e32683","timestamp"